you be subjective, whatever you want it to be. But it still mathematically imposes restrictions

6
00:01:01,160 --> 00:01:06,920
on how you have to update your beliefs when new evidence comes in. So there is the conditional

7
00:01:06,920 --> 00:01:12,160
probability formula, which says the probability of A given B, and whenever you see a probability

8
00:01:12,160 --> 00:01:17,480
P with A and a vertical line and another one, always read that probability of A given B.

9
00:01:17,480 --> 00:01:24,080
So probability of A given B is equal to the probability that A and B happen divided by

10
00:01:24,080 --> 00:01:28,560
the probability of B. So for example, the probability that you get two heads given that

11
00:01:28,560 --> 00:01:33,000
the first flip of a coin is heads equals the probability of two heads divided by the probability

12
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:40,000
of the first flip is heads, which equals 0.25 over 0.5, which equals 0.5. Then of course

13
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:47,160
we can apply this to more fanciful examples like, how about the probability that a child

14
00:01:47,160 --> 00:01:51,520
really saw a monster given that he says he saw a monster. So you may have heard children

15
00:01:51,520 --> 00:01:55,000
claim to see monsters at some point. You may have even claimed to have seen a monster at

16
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:00,600
some point. So what's the probability that he really did given that he says he did? So

17
00:02:00,600 --> 00:02:04,160
this is equal to the probability that he saw a monster and there was a monster divided

18
00:02:04,160 --> 00:02:07,680
by the probability that he says he saw a monster. So for example, the probability that he saw

19
00:02:07,680 --> 00:02:14,400
a monster and there is a monster is one in a billion. So one in a billion, and that seems

20
00:02:14,400 --> 00:02:18,440
if anything low. There's all kinds of weird things the kid might encounter. So what is

